Sofia","key":"ananievapau","last":"Ananieva","name":"Ananieva, Sofia","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Sandra","key":"greinerpau","last":"Greiner","name":"Greiner, Sandra","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Jacob","key":"krügerpau","last":"Krüger","name":"Krüger, Jacob","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Lukas","key":"linsbauerpau","last":"Linsbauer","name":"Linsbauer, Lukas","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Sten","key":"grünerpau","last":"Grüner","name":"Grüner, Sten","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Timo","key":"kehrerpau","last":"Kehrer","name":"Kehrer, Timo","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas","key":"kühnpau","last":"Kühn","name":"Kühn, Thomas","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christoph","key":"seidlpau","last":"Seidl","locid":"fda8c630-1b0a-4910-ae2a-602850616275","name":"Seidl, Christoph","orcid":"0000-0003-4539-8297","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Ralf","key":"reussnerpau","last":"Reussner","name":"Reussner, Ralf","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3510466.3510483","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the International Working Conference on Variability Modelling of Software-Intensive Systems (VaMoS), 2022"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85125374854"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-def1275e-d44f-465c-8a7b-f5682d10c6af"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Unified Operations for Variability in Space and Time","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384545","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"In model-based software engineering, models are central artifacts for management, design and implementation. To meet new requirements, engineers need to plan and perform model evolution. So far, model evolution histories are captured using version control systems, e.g., Git. However, these systems are unsuitable for planning model evolution as they do not have a notion of future changes. Furthermore, formally assigning responsibilities to engineers for performing evolution of model parts is achieved by using additional tools for access control. To remedy these shortcomings, we provide a method to generate evolution-aware modeling notations by augmenting existing metamodels with concepts for capturing previous performed and planned evolution as first-class entity. To provide a clear overview, we automatically generate a Gantt-style viewer for augmented models and capabilities to slice models for certain time periods. Our method enables engineers to seamlessly plan future model evolution while actively developing the current model state using a centralized access point for evolution. With the generated Gantt-style viewers and the slicing functionality, we enable engineers to inspect relevant model evolution while reducing model size and hiding unnecessary complexity. In our evaluation, we provide an implementation of our method in the tool TemporalRegulator3000. We show applicability for real-world metamodels and capture the entire evolution timeline of corresponding models.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Nieke, Michael","Hoff, Adrian","Seidl, Christoph","Schaefer, Ina"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384297"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Technical University of Braunschweig"],"keyword":["Evolution","History","MODEL","Metamodel","Model based Software Engineering","Planning","Timeline"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"augmenting metamodels with seamless support for planning tracking and slicing model evolution timelines","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cola.2021.101031","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384297","identifier":[{"id":"85103769263","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-cebdd428-b82c-48c2-b07c-195d757ecbed"],"keyword":["Evolution","History","Metamodel","Model","Model Based Software Engineering","Planning","Timeline"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-018366AD06C988DC829FAECF93CAB0B7"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cola.2021.101031","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86412113/AugmentingMetamodelsWithSeamlessSupport_alt.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"technical university of braunschweig","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Technical University of Braunschweig","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"niekepau","last":"Nieke","name":"Nieke, Michael","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Adrian","key":"hoffpau","last":"Hoff","name":"Hoff, Adrian","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Christoph","key":"seidlpau","last":"Seidl","locid":"fda8c630-1b0a-4910-ae2a-602850616275","name":"Seidl, Christoph","orcid":"0000-0003-4539-8297","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ina","key":"schaeferpau","last":"Schaefer","name":"Schaefer, Ina","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.cola.2021.101031","issn":["25901184"],"journal":"Journal of Computer Languages","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Journal of Computer Languages"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85103769263"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-cebdd428-b82c-48c2-b07c-195d757ecbed"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Augmenting Metamodels with Seamless Support for Planning, Tracking, and Slicing Model Evolution Timelines","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384297","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Many of today’s software systems will become the legacy systems of tomorrow, comprised of outdated technology and inaccurate design documents. Preparing for their eventual re-engineering requires engineers to regain lost design knowledge and discover re-engineering opportunities. While tools and visualizations exist, comprehending an unfamiliar code base remains challenging. Hence, software archaeology suffers from a considerable entry barrier as it requires expert knowledge, significant diligence, tenacity, and stamina. In this paper, we propose a paradigm shift in how legacy systems’ design knowledge can be regained by presenting our vision for an immersive explorable software visualization in virtual reality (VR). We propose innovative concepts leveraging benefits of VR for a) immersion in an exoteric visualization metaphor, b) effective navigation and orientation, c) guiding exploration, and d) maintaining a link to the implementation. By enabling immersive and playful legacy system exploration, we strive for lowering the entry barrier, fostering long-term engagement, strengthening mental-model building, and improving knowledge retention in an effort to ease coping with the increased number of tomorrow’s legacy systems.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Hoff, Adrian","Nieke, Michael","Seidl, Christoph"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381714"],"event":{"acronym":"ESEC/FSE","title":"ACM Joint European Software Engineering Conference and Symposium on the Foundations of Software Engineering (ESEC/FSE)"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Legacy Software","Software Archaeology","Software Engineering","Software Re Engineering","Software Visualization","Virtual reality"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"towards immersive software archaeology regaining legacy systems design knowledge via interactive exploration in virtual reality","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3468264.3473128","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381714","identifier":[{"id":"85116236681","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-1e24f8ee-a993-4388-b210-ed5fc39ba659"],"keyword":["Legacy Software","Software Archaeology","Software Engineering","Software Re-Engineering","Software Visualization","Virtual Reality"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-E82353C3DFD14B4EB5A9E47FDAD7BD66"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86190732/fse21_21_07_07_camera_ready_authors_version.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3468264.3473128","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Adrian","key":"hoffpau","last":"Hoff","locid":"6a57a243-af32-4e1d-849c-75146da60102","name":"Hoff, Adrian","orcid":"0000-0002-5254-6246","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"niekepau","last":"Nieke","name":"Nieke, Michael","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christoph","key":"seidlpau","last":"Seidl","locid":"fda8c630-1b0a-4910-ae2a-602850616275","name":"Seidl, Christoph","orcid":"0000-0003-4539-8297","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3468264.3473128","pages":"1455-1458","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 29th ACM Joint European Software Engineering Conference and Symposium on the Foundations of Software Engineering (ESEC/FSE ’21)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85116236681"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-1e24f8ee-a993-4388-b210-ed5fc39ba659"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Towards Immersive Software Archaeology: Regaining Legacy Systems’ Design Knowledge via Interactive Exploration in Virtual Reality","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381714","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Domain-specific languages seek to provide domain guarantees that eliminate many errors allowed by general-purpose languages. Still, a domain-specific language requires additional quality assurance measures to ensure that specifications behave as intended by the users. However, some domains may have specific quality assurance measures (e.g., proofs, experiments, or case studies) with little tradition of using quality assurance measures customary to software engineering. We investigate the possibility of accommodating such domains by conducting a workshop with 11 prospective users of a domain-specific language named MAL for the pension industry. The workshop emphasised the need for supporting actuaries with new analytical tools for quality assurance and resulted in three designs: quantity monitors let users identify outlier behaviour, fragment debugging lets users debug with limited evaluative power, and debugging spreadsheets let users visualise, analyse, and remodel concrete calculations with an established domain tool. Based on our experiences, we hypothesise that co-design workshops are a viable approach for DSLs in a similar situation.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Borum, Holger Stadel","Seidl, Christoph","Sestoft, Peter"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383903"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Actuarial Analysis Tools","Co Design Workshops","Domain specific languages","Pension Industry","Quality assurance"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"co designing dsl quality assurance measures for and with non programming experts","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86333743/holger_splash_dsm21.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-383903","ids":["itu-b5037a03-dbae-4391-9177-8b8f6278b1c5"],"keyword":["Actuarial Analysis Tools","Co-Design Workshops","Domain-Specific Languages","Pension Industry","Quality Assurance"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-4-8-D513EA10EAB04C349196E4478FB0BEAA"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86333743/holger_splash_dsm21.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Software Engineering, Software Quality Research"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Holger Stadel","key":"borumpau","last":"Borum","name":"Borum, Holger Stadel","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christoph","key":"seidlpau","last":"Seidl","locid":"fda8c630-1b0a-4910-ae2a-602850616275","name":"Seidl, Christoph","orcid":"0000-0003-4539-8297","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Peter","key":"sestoftpau","last":"Sestoft","locid":"0ee7ba1e-3108-4db3-a908-97977980d032","name":"Sestoft, Peter","orcid":"0000-0002-5843-6021","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3486603.3486776","eisbn":["9781450391061"],"pages":"31-40","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"DSM 2021: Proceedings of the 18th ACM SIGPLAN International Workshop on Domain-Specific Modeling"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85118868490","2-s2.0-85118868545"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-b5037a03-dbae-4391-9177-8b8f6278b1c5"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Co-designing DSL quality assurance measures for and with non-programming experts","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383903","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"A feature model represents a set of variants as configurable features and dependencies between them. During variant configuration, (de)selection of a feature may entail that other features must or cannot be selected. A Modal Implication Graph (MIG) enables efficient decision propagation to perform automatic (de)selection of subsequent features. In addition, it facilitates other configuration-related activities such as t-wise sampling. Evolution of a feature model may change its configuration logic, thereby invalidating an existing MIG and forcing a full recomputation. However, repeated recomputation of a MIG is expensive, and thus hampers the overall usefulness of MIGs for frequently evolving feature models. In this paper, we devise a method to incrementally compute updated MIGs after feature model evolution. We identify expensive steps in the MIG construction algorithm, enable them for incremental computation, and measure performance compared to a full rebuild of a complete MIG within the evolution histories of four real-world feature models. Results show that our incremental method can increase the speed of MIG construction by orders of magnitude, depending on the given scenario and extent of evolutionary changes.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Krieter, Sebastian","Arens, Rahel","Nieke, Michael","Sundermann, Chico","Heß, Tobias","Thüm, Thomas","Seidl, Christoph"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384051"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Harz University of Applied Sciences","Technical University of Braunschweig","Ulm University"],"keyword":["Configuration Logic Evolution","Feature Model","Incremental Computation","Modal Implication Graph MIG","Variant Configuration"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"incremental construction of modal implication graphs for evolving feature models","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3461001.3471148","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384051","identifier":[{"id":"85115309019","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-be0323b9-e678-4417-b402-f19d96aaae2e"],"keyword":["Configuration Logic Evolution","Feature Model","Incremental Computation","Modal Implication Graph (MIG)","Variant Configuration"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-0E3E2A1FC7C4FEA45C9C249547B52A54"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86199118/_.._SPLC_21_Incremental_Construction_of_Modal_Implication_Graphs_for_Evolving_Feature_Models_S._Krieter_R._Arens_M._Nieke_C._Sundermann_T._He_T._Th_m_C._Seidl_2021_.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3461001.3471148","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86522791/_C37_SPLC_21_Incremental_Construction_of_Modal_Implication_Graphs_for_Evolving_Feature_Models_S._Krieter_R._Arens_M._Nieke_C._Sundermann_T._He_T._Th_m_C._Seidl_2021_.pdf","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"harz university of applied sciences","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Harz University of Applied Sciences","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"technical university of braunschweig","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Technical University of Braunschweig","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"de","key":"ulm university","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Ulm University","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sebastian","key":"krieterpau","last":"Krieter","name":"Krieter, Sebastian","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Rahel","key":"arenspau","last":"Arens","name":"Arens, Rahel","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"niekepau","last":"Nieke","name":"Nieke, Michael","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Chico","key":"sundermannpau","last":"Sundermann","name":"Sundermann, Chico","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Tobias","key":"heßpau","last":"Heß","name":"Heß, Tobias","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas","key":"thümpau","last":"Thüm","name":"Thüm, Thomas","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Christoph","key":"seidlpau","last":"Seidl","locid":"fda8c630-1b0a-4910-ae2a-602850616275","name":"Seidl, Christoph","orcid":"0000-0003-4539-8297","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3461001.3471148","pages":"64-74","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 25th ACM International Systems and Software Product Line Conference (SPLC'21) - Volume A"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85115309019"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-be0323b9-e678-4417-b402-f19d96aaae2e"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Incremental Construction of Modal Implication Graphs for Evolving Feature Models","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&