e":"Seidl, Maximilian","rank":45,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Vassilios","key":"lougarispau","last":"Lougaris","name":"Lougaris, Vassilios","rank":46,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Alessandro","key":"plebanipau","last":"Plebani","name":"Plebani, Alessandro","rank":47,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"31","corresponding":null,"first":"Laia","key":"alsinapau","last":"Alsina","name":"Alsina, Laia","rank":48,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"32","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mette","key":"christiansenpau","last":"Christiansen","locid":"db9a5fac-4160-421d-b6aa-dcda1847bc02","name":"Christiansen, Mette","orcid":"0000-0001-5158-3670","rank":49,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["32278790"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.jaci.2019.11.051","issn":["00916749","10858725"],"issn_org":["00916749"],"issue":"4","journal":"Journal of Allergy and Clinical Immunology","pages":"901-911","publisher":"Elsevier Inc.","status":"p","title":"Journal of Allergy and Clinical Immunology","volume":"146"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85084240212"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-c5ea811b-03ff-4301-9042-30fd0b252ec3"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Characterization of the clinical and immunologic phenotype and management of 157 individuals with 56 distinct heterozygous <i>NFKB1 </i>mutations","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-242330","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"In immersive augmented reality (IAR), users can wear a head-mounted display to see computer-generated images superimposed to their view of the world. IAR was shown to be beneficial across several domains, e.g., automotive, medicine, gaming and engineering, with positive impacts on, e.g., collaboration and communication. We think that IAR bears a great potential for software engineering but, as of yet, this research area has been neglected. In this vision paper, we elicit potentials and obstacles for the use of IAR in software engineering. We identify possible areas that can be supported with IAR technology by relating commonly discussed IAR improvements to typical software engineering tasks. We further demonstrate how innovative use of IAR technology may fundamentally improve typical activities of a software engineer through a comprehensive series of usage scenarios outlining practical application. Finally, we reflect on current limitations of IAR technology based on our scenarios and sketch research activities necessary to make our vision a reality. We consider this paper to be relevant to academia and industry alike in guiding the steps to innovative research and applications for IAR in software engineering.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Merino, Leonel","Lungu, Mircea","Seidl, Christoph"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382130"],"event":{"acronym":"SANER","title":"IEEE International Conference on Software Analysis, Evolution and Reengineering"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Stuttgart"],"keyword":["Software Engineering","Virtual reality"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"unleashing the potentials of immersive augmented reality for software engineering","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85506912/2001.01223.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-382130","identifier":[{"id":"85083593778","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-3ae8a471-9418-461a-bfa7-b95c94579cea"],"keyword":["software engineering","virtual reality"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-96F346964CB5A6887211286B4D520510"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85506912/2001.01223.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"university of stuttgart","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Stuttgart","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Software Engineering, Software Engineering"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Leonel","key":"merinopau","last":"Merino","name":"Merino, Leonel","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Mircea","key":"lungupau","last":"Lungu","locid":"02960d47-7677-4b2c-99d4-490a31fd6885","name":"Lungu, Mircea","orcid":"0000-0003-3944-5261","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Christoph","key":"seidlpau","last":"Seidl","locid":"fda8c630-1b0a-4910-ae2a-602850616275","name":"Seidl, Christoph","orcid":"0000-0003-4539-8297","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/saner48275.2020.9054812","publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of The 27th IEEE International Conference on Software Analysis, Evolution and Reengineering (SANER)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85083593778"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-3ae8a471-9418-461a-bfa7-b95c94579cea"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Unleashing the Potentials of Immersive Augmented Reality for Software Engineering","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382130","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Families of related MATLAB/Simulink systems commonly emerge ad hoc using clone-and-own practices. Extractively migrating systems towards a software product line (SPL) can be a remedy. A feature model (FM) represents all potential configurations of an SPL, ideally, in non-technical domain terms. However, yielding a sensible FM from automated synthesis remains a major challenge due to domain knowledge being a prerequisite for features to be adequate abstractions. In incremental reverse engineering, subsequent generation of FMs may further overwrite changes and design decisions made during previous manual FM refinement. In this paper, we propose an approach to largely automate the synthesis of a suitable FM from a set of cloned MATLAB/Simulink models as part of reverse engineering an SPL. We fully automate the extraction of an initial, i.e., a technical, FM that closely aligns with realization artifacts and their variability, and further provide operations to manually refine it to incorporate domain knowledge. Most importantly, we provide concepts to capture such operations and to replay them on a structurally different technical FM stemming from a subsequent reverse engineering increment that included further systems of the portfolio. We further provide an implementation and demonstrate the feasibility of our approach using two MATLAB/Simulink data sets from the automotive domain.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Schlie, Alexander","Knüppel, Alexander","Seidl, Christoph","Schaefer, Ina"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382625"],"event":{"acronym":"SPLC","title":"International Systems and Software Product Line Conference"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Technical University of Braunschweig"],"keyword":["Clone and own practices","Feature model synthesis","MATLAB Simulink systems","Reverse engineering","Software product line SPL"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"incremental feature model synthesis for clone and own software systems in matlab simulink","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85551491/Incremental_Feature_Model_Synthesis.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382625","identifier":[{"id":"85097821921","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-5b5811d2-f7a0-4ad7-956d-01f305d93d0f"],"keyword":["Clone-and-own practices","Feature model synthesis","MATLAB/Simulink systems","Reverse engineering","Software product line (SPL)"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-62635DC067BF803D1F95039024090B4B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85551491/Incremental_Feature_Model_Synthesis.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"technical university of braunschweig","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Technical University of Braunschweig","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Alexander","key":"schliepau","last":"Schlie","name":"Schlie, Alexander","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Alexander","key":"knüppelpau","last":"Knüppel","name":"Knüppel, Alexander","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Christoph","key":"seidlpau","last":"Seidl","locid":"fda8c630-1b0a-4910-ae2a-602850616275","name":"Seidl, Christoph","orcid":"0000-0003-4539-8297","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ina","key":"schaeferpau","last":"Schaefer","name":"Schaefer, Ina","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3382025.3414973","pages":"1-12","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 24th ACM International Systems and Software Product Line Conference"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85097821921"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-5b5811d2-f7a0-4ad7-956d-01f305d93d0f"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Incremental Feature Model Synthesis for Clone-and-Own Software Systems in MATLAB/Simulink","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382625","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"A product line is an approach for systematically managing configuration options of customizable systems, usually by means of features. Products are generated by utilizing configurations consisting of selected features. Product-line evolution can lead to unintended changes to product behavior. We illustrate that updating configurations after product-line evolution requires decisions of both, domain engineers responsible for product-line evolution as well as application engineers responsible for configurations. The challenge is that domain and application engineers might not be able to talk to each other. We propose a formal foundation and a methodology that enables domain engineers to guide application engineers through configuration evolution by sharing knowledge on product-line evolution and by defining configuration update operations. As an effect, we enable knowledge transfer between those engineers without the need to talk to each other. We evaluate our method by providing formal proofs that show product behavior of configurations can be preserved for typical evolution scenarios.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Nieke, Michael","Sampaio, Gabriela","Thüm, Thomas","Seidl, Christoph","Teixeira, Leopoldo","Schaefer, Ina"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383858"],"event":{"acronym":"SPLC","title":"International Systems and Software Product Line Conference"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Configuration management","Domain and application engineering collaboration","Feature based customization","Product line engineering","Product line evolution"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"guydance guiding configuration updates for product line evolution","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85551485/GuyDance.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383858","identifier":[{"id":"85097632294","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-b177c3e4-7231-48ba-aaf8-93adcf10f4e4"],"keyword":["Configuration management","Domain and application engineering collaboration","Feature-based customization","Product line engineering","Product-line evolution"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-61094F7E54162FDB6BB15CA242BE3C5D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85551485/GuyDance.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"niekepau","last":"Nieke","name":"Nieke, Michael","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Gabriela","key":"sampaiopau","last":"Sampaio","name":"Sampaio, Gabriela","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas","key":"thümpau","last":"Thüm","name":"Thüm, Thomas","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christoph","key":"seidlpau","last":"Seidl","locid":"fda8c630-1b0a-4910-ae2a-602850616275","name":"Seidl, Christoph","orcid":"0000-0003-4539-8297","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Leopoldo","key":"teixeirapau","last":"Teixeira","name":"Teixeira, Leopoldo","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Ina","key":"schaeferpau","last":"Schaefer","name":"Schaefer, Ina","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3382026.3425769","pages":"56-64","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 24th ACM International Systems and Software Product Line Conference"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85097632294"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-b177c3e4-7231-48ba-aaf8-93adcf10f4e4"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"GuyDance: Guiding Configuration Updates for Product-Line Evolution","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383858","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"A software product line (SPL) enables large-scale reuse in a family of related software systems through configurable features. SPLs represent a long-term investment so that their ongoing evolution becomes paramount and requires careful planning. While existing approaches enable to create an evolution plan for an SPL on feature-model (FM) level, they assume the plan to be rigid and do not support retroactive changes. In this paper, we present a method that enables to create and retroactively adapt an FM evolution plan while preventing undesired impacts on its structural and logical consistency. This method is founded in structural operational semantics and linear temporal logic. We implement our method using rewriting logic, integrate it within an FM tool suite and perform an evaluation using a collection of existing FM evolution scenarios.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Hoff, Adrian","Nieke, Michael","Seidl, Christoph","Halvard Sæther, Eirik","Sandberg Motzfeldt, Ida","Din, Crystal Chang","Yu, Ingrid Chieh","Schaefer, Ina"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384089"],"event":{"acronym":"SPLC","title":"International Systems and Software Product Line Conference"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Germany","Norway"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Technical University of Braunschweig","University of Oslo"],"keyword":["Evolution Plan","Feature Model","Linear Temporal Logic","Software Product Line","Structural Operational Semantics"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"consistency preserving evolution planning on feature models","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3382025.3414964","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384089","identifier":[{"id":"85097836099","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-c1483451-6ccf-4f9a-a73d-3434cdb39246"],"keyword":["Evolution Plan","Feature Model","Linear Temporal Logic","Software Product Line","Structural Operational Semantics"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-9EEADD46ADDAE5BDBDFEA1937110BED8"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85551380/Consistency_preserving_evolution_planning_on_feature_models.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3382025.3414964","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"technical university of braunschweig","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Technical University of Braunschweig","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"no","key":"university of oslo","loc":["Norway","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"University of Oslo","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Adrian","key":"hoffpau","last":"Hoff","locid":"6a57a243-af32-4e1d-849c-75146da60102","name":"Hoff, Adrian","orcid":"0000-0002-5254-6246","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"niekepau","last":"Nieke","name":"Nieke, Michael","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christoph","key":"seidlpau","last":"Seidl","locid":"fda8c630-1b0a-4910-ae2a-602850616275","name":"Seidl, Christoph","orcid":"0000-0003-4539-8297","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Eirik","key":"halvardsætherpau","last":"Halvard Sæther","name":"Halvard Sæther, Eirik","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Ida","key":"sandbergmotzfeldtpau","last":"Sandberg Motzfeldt","name":"Sandberg Motzfeldt, Ida","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Crystal Chang","key":"dinpau","last":"Din","name":"Din, Crystal Chang","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Ingrid Chieh","key":"yupau","last":"Yu","name":"Yu, Ingrid Chieh","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Ina","key":"schaeferpau","last":"Schaefer","name":"Schaefer, Ina","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3382025.3414964","eisbn":["9781450375696"],"pages":"1-12","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 24th ACM International Systems and Software Product Line Conference"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85097836099"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-c1483451-6ccf-4f9a-a73d-3434cdb39246"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Consistency-Preserving Evolution Planning on Feature Models","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384089","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"In Model-Driven Software Development, models are processed automatically to support the creation, build, and execution of systems. A large variety of dedicated model-transformation languages exists, promising to efficiently realize the automated processing of models. To investigate the actual benefit of using such specialized languages, we performed a large-scale controlled experiment in which 78 subjects solved 231 individual tasks using three languages. The experiment sheds light on commonalities and differences between model transformation languages (ATL, QVT-O) and on benefits of using them in common development tasks (comprehension, change, and creation) against a modern general-purpose language (Xtend). The results of our experiment show no statistically significant benefit of using a dedicated transformation language over a modern general-purpose language. However, we were able to identify several aspects of transformation programming where domain-specific transformation languages do appear to help, including copying objects, context identification, and conditioning the computation on types.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Hebig, Regina","Seidl, Christoph","Berger, Thorsten","Kook Pedersen, John","Wasowski, Andrzej"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382698"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Germany","Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Chalmers University of Technology","Technical University of Braunschweig","University of Gothenburg"],"keyword":["ATL","Comprehension Tasks","Controlled experiment","Domain specific languages","General Purpose Language","Model Driven Software Development","Model Transformation Languages","QVT O","Transformation Programming","Xtend"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"model transformation languages under a magnifying glass a controlled experiment with xtend atl and qvt","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.18420/se2019-25","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382698","identifier":[{"id":"85072132568","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-5efff7a9-6de2-4c42-bfa5-6e634d75e6fc"],"keyword":["ATL","Comprehension Tasks","Controlled Experiment","Domain-Specific Languages","General-Purpose Language","Model Transformation Languages","Model-Driven Software Development","QVT-O","Transformation Programming","Xtend"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-CB145ECB8C221D36C7FC5F7228D12A86"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.18420/se2019-25","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84798921/25.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"se","key":"chalmers university of technology","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Chalmers University of Technology","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"se","key":"university of gothenburg","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"University of Gothenburg","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"de","key":"technical university of braunschweig","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Technical University of Braunschweig","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Software Engineering, Software Quality Research"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Regina","key":"hebigpau","last":"Hebig","name":"Hebig, Regina","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Christoph","key":"seidlpau","last":"Seidl","name":"Seidl, Christoph","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Thorsten","key":"bergerpau","last":"Berger","name":"Berger, Thorsten","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"John","key":"kookpedersenpau","last":"Kook Pedersen","name":"Kook Pedersen, John","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Andrzej","key":"wasowskipau","last":"Wasowski","locid":"a39b94ff-e1b6-4b96-8404-486167a9123d","name":"Wasowski, Andrzej","orcid":"0000-0003-0532-2685","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.18420/se2019-25","eisbn":["9783885796862"],"issn":["16175468"],"pages":"91-92","publisher":"Gesellschaft für Informatik","series":"Lecture Notes in Informatics","status":"p","title":"Software Engineering and Software Management, SE/SWM 2019, Stuttgart, Germany, February 18-22, 2019"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85072132568"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-5efff7a9-6de2-4c42-bfa5-6e634d75e6fc"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Model Transformation Languages under a Magnifying Glass: A Controlled Experiment with Xtend, ATL, and QVT","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382698","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Software Product Lines (SPLs) are a mechanism for large-scale reuse where families of related software systems are represented in terms of commonalities and variabilities, e.g., using Feature Models (FMs). While FMs define all possible configurations of an SPL, when considering dynamic SPLs and environmental conditions, not every possible configuration may be valid in all possible contexts. A change in the environment may, therefore, require the reconfiguration of the SPL. With common modeling methodologies, it is not possible to capture the correlation of configuration options, contextual influences, user customizations, and evolution. In this paper, we remedy this problem by first defining a novel framework that allows modeling customizable evolving context-aware SPLs. We then provide a reconfiguration engine that computes how the current configuration needs to be reconfigured when the context is altered, the user preferences changed or the SPL artifacts are evolved and the configuration is adapted to reflect the evolved artifacts.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Mauro, Jacopo","Nieke, Michael","Seidl, Christoph","Chieh Yu, Ingrid"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-589846"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Mauro, Jacopo"],"country":["Germany","Norway"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Braunschweig University of Technology","University of Oslo"],"keyword":["Context aware","Optimization","Preferences"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"context aware reconfiguration in evolving software product lines","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/143276636/Context_aware_reconfiguration_in_evolving_software_product_lines.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["This work was supported by the European project HyVar (grant agreement H2020-644298 ), by the Federal Ministry of Education and Research of Germany within the project CrESt (funding number 01IS16043S ) and by the DFG ( German Research Foundation ) under the Priority Programme SPP1593 : Design For Future — Managed Software Evolution."],"id":"S01-000.00-589846","identifier":[{"id":"85047275609","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-3a22c8ca-2252-4594-bbbb-dd423d571a39"],"keyword":["Context-aware","Optimization","Preferences","SPL"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-3D32C1B9FF13C158FA7F8B5493C2C8CE"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/143276636/Context_aware_reconfiguration_in_evolving_software_product_lines.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, Faculty of Science, Department of Mathematics and Computer Science","SDU, Faculty of Science, Department of Mathematics and Computer Science, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"braunschweig university of technology","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Braunschweig University of Technology","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"no","key":"university of oslo","loc":["Norway","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"University of Oslo","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jacopo","key":"mauropau","last":"Mauro","locid":"ad1ad59c-7294-44a8-98d6-d5329b94baac","name":"Mauro, Jacopo","orcid":"0000-0002-5285-2868","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"niekepau","last":"Nieke","name":"Nieke, Michael","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Christoph","key":"seidlpau","last":"Seidl","name":"Seidl, Christoph","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Ingrid","key":"chiehyupau","last":"Chieh Yu","name":"Chieh Yu, Ingrid","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.scico.2018.05.002","issn":["01676423","18727964"],"issn_org":["01676423"],"journal":"Science of Computer Programming","pages":"139-159","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Science of Computer Programming","volume":"163"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85047275609"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-3a22c8ca-2252-4594-bbbb-dd423d571a39"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Context-aware reconfiguration in evolving software product lines","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-589846","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Software Product Lines (SPLs) are a common technique to capture families of software products in terms of commonalities and variabilities. On a conceptual level, functionality of an SPL is modeled in terms of features in Feature Models (FMs). As other software systems, SPLs and their FMs are subject to evolution that may lead to the introduction of anomalies (e.g., non-selectable features). To fix such anomalies, developers need to understand the cause for them. However, for large evolution histories and large SPLs, explanations may become very long and, as a consequence, hard to understand. In this paper, we present a method for anomaly detection and explanation that, by encoding the entire evolution history, identifies the evolution step of anomaly introduction and explains which of the performed evolution operations lead to it. In our evaluation, we show that our method significantly reduces the complexity of generated explanations.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Nieke, Michael","Thüm, Thomas","Mauro, Jacopo","Yu, Ingrid Chieh","Seidl, Christoph","Franzke, Felix"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-626566"],"event":{"acronym":"GPCE","end":"2018-11-06","place":"Boston, us","start":"2018-11-05","title":"17th ACM SIGPLAN International Conference on Generative Programming: Concepts and Experiences, GPCE 2018, co-located with SPLASH 2018","year":"2018"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Germany","Norway"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Braunschweig University of Technology","University of Oslo"],"keyword":["Anomalies","Evolution","Explanation","Feature Model","Software Product Line"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"anomaly analyses for feature model evolution","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85059023955","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-626566","identifier":[{"id":"85059023955","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-ba20ce67-d046-47a8-9a94-f6d62c724ca1"],"keyword":["Anomalies","Evolution","Evolution Operation","Explanation","Feature Model","Software Product Line"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-6-5BBA2C882A0B3E6DB26BC4928162F1C9"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"braunschweig university of technology","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Braunschweig University of Technology","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, Faculty of Science, Department of Mathematics and Computer Science","SDU, Faculty of Science, Department of Mathematics and Computer Science, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"no","key":"university of oslo","loc":["Norway","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"University of Oslo","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"niekepau","last":"Nieke","name":"Nieke, Michael","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas","key":"thümpau","last":"Thüm","name":"Thüm, Thomas","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jacopo","key":"mauropau","last":"Mauro","locid":"ad1ad59c-7294-44a8-98d6-d5329b94baac","name":"Mauro, Jacopo","orcid":"0000-0002-5285-2868","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Ingrid Chieh","key":"yupau","last":"Yu","name":"Yu, Ingrid Chieh","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christoph","key":"seidlpau","last":"Seidl","name":"Seidl, Christoph","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Felix","key":"franzkepau","last":"Franzke","name":"Franzke, Felix","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Eric","key":"vanwykpedh","last":"Van Wyk","name":"Van Wyk, Eric","rank":7,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Tiark","key":"rompfpedh","last":"Rompf","name":"Rompf, Tiark","rank":8,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3393934.3278123","eisbn":["9781450360456"],"pages":"188-201","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","subtitle":"Concepts and Experiences, co-located with SPLASH 2018","title":"GPCE 2018 - Proceedings of the 17th ACM SIGPLAN International Conference on Generative Programming"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85059023955"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-ba20ce67-d046-47a8-9a94-f6d62c724ca1"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Anomaly analyses for feature-model evolution","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-626566","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"In Model-Driven Software Development, models are automatically processed to support the creation, build, and execution of systems. A large variety of dedicated model-transformation languages exists, promising to efficiently realize the automated processing of models. To investigate the actual benefit of using such specialized languages, we performed a large-scale controlled experiment in which over 78 subjects solve 231 individual tasks using three languages. The experiment sheds light on commonalities and differences between model transformation languages (ATL, QVT-O) and on benefits of using them in common development tasks (comprehension, change, and creation) against a modern general-purpose language (Xtend). Our results show no statistically significant benefit of using a dedicated transformation language over a modern general-purpose language. However, we were able to identify several aspects of transformation programming where domain-specific transformation languages do appear to help, including copying objects, context identification, and conditioning the computation on types.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Hebig, Regina","Berger, Thorsten","Seidl, Christoph","Kook Pedersen, John","Wasowski, Andrzej"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381608"],"event":{"acronym":"ESEC/FSE","title":"ACM Joint European Software Engineering Conference and Symposium on the Foundations of Software Engineering (ESEC/FSE)"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Germany","Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Chalmers University of Technology","Technical University of Braunschweig","University of Gothenburg"],"keyword":["ATL","Change Tasks","Comprehension Tasks","Copying Objects","Domain specific languages","General Purpose Languages","Model Driven Software Development","Model Transformation Languages","QVT O","Transformation Programming"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"model transformation languages under a magnifying glass a controlled experiment with xtend atl and qvt","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83700789/2018_fse_model_trafos_regina_thorsten_christoph.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381608","identifier":[{"id":"85058341895","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-168e395e-91c5-4d37-8276-9477539eab85"],"keyword":["ATL","Change Tasks","Comprehension Tasks","Copying Objects","Domain-Specific Languages","General-Purpose Languages","Model Transformation Languages","Model-Driven Software Development","QVT-O","Transformation Programming"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-929F2A7298F7621E91176A11CA49B87F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83700789/2018_fse_model_trafos_regina_thorsten_christoph.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"se","key":"chalmers university of technology","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Chalmers University of Technology","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"se","key":"university of gothenburg","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"University of Gothenburg","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"de","key":"technical university of braunschweig","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Technical University of Braunschweig","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Software Engineering, Software Quality Research"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Regina","key":"hebigpau","last":"Hebig","name":"Hebig, Regina","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Thorsten","key":"bergerpau","last":"Berger","name":"Berger, Thorsten","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Christoph","key":"seidlpau","last":"Seidl","name":"Seidl, Christoph","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"John","key":"kookpedersenpau","last":"Kook Pedersen","name":"Kook Pedersen, John","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Andrzej","key":"wasowskipau","last":"Wasowski","locid":"a39b94ff-e1b6-4b96-8404-486167a9123d","name":"Wasowski, Andrzej","orcid":"0000-0003-0532-2685","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3236024.3236046","isbn":["9781450355735"],"pages":"445-455","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 2018 ACM Joint Meeting on European Software Engineering Conference and Symposium on the Foundations of Software Engineering, ESEC/SIGSOFT FSE 2018, Lake Buena Vista, FL, USA, November 04-09, 2018"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85058341895"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-168e395e-91c5-4d37-8276-9477539eab85"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Model transformation languages under a magnifying glass: a controlled experiment with Xtend, ATL, and QVT","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381608","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Granulomas are immune cell aggregates formed in response to persistent inflammatory stimuli. Granuloma macrophage subsets are diverse and carry varying copy numbers of their genomic information. The molecular programs that control the differentiation of such macrophage populations in response to a chronic stimulus, though critical for disease outcome, have not been defined. Here, we delineate a macrophage differentiation pathway by which a persistent Toll-like receptor (TLR) 2 signal instructs polyploid macrophage fate by inducing replication stress and activating the DNA damage response. Polyploid granuloma-resident macrophages formed via modified cell divisions and mitotic defects and not, as previously thought, by cell-to-cell fusion. TLR2 signaling promoted macrophage polyploidy and suppressed genomic instability by regulating Myc and ATR. We propose that, in the presence of persistent inflammatory stimuli, pathways previously linked to oncogene-initiated carcinogenesis instruct a long-lived granuloma-resident macrophage differentiation program that regulates granulomatous tissue remodeling.","author_count":38,"contributors":["Herrtwich, Laura","Nanda, Indrajit","Evangelou, Konstantinos","Nikolova, Teodora","Horn, Veronika","Sagar, no-firstname","Erny, Daniel","Stefanowski, Jonathan","Rogell, Leif","Klein, Claudius","Gharun, Kourosh","Follo, Marie","Seidl, Maximilian","Kremer, Bernhard","Muenke, Nikolas","Senges, Julia","Fliegauf, Manfred","Aschman, Tom","Pfeifer, Dietmar","Sarrazin, Sandrine","Sieweke, Michael H.","Wagner, Dirk","Dierks, Christine","Haaf, Thomas","Ness, Thomas","Zaiss, Mario M.","Voll, Reinhard E.","Deshmukh, Sachin D.","Prinz, Marco","Goldmann, Torsten","Hoelscher, Christoph","Hauser, Anja E.","Lopez-Contreras, Andres J.","Gruen, Dominic","Gorgoulis, Vassilis","Diefenbach, Andreas","Henneke, Philipp","Triantafyllopoulou, Antigoni"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-424760"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"dna damage signaling instructs polyploid macrophage fate in granulomas","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-424760","identifier":[{"id":"000389470100018","type":"wos"},{"id":"84995961338","type":"scopus"},{"id":"28084216","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-34bce7cb-a03b-4c2d-974d-5633220e4db9"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-8BA87076AE856E3E955B19FCE76DA8F0"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Center for Healthy Ageing, Web Track 1: Modifiable pathways of damage and repair in aging","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Cellular and Molecular Medicine, Molecular Aging Program"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Laura","key":"herrtwichpau","last":"Herrtwich","name":"Herrtwich, Laura","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Indrajit","key":"nandapau","last":"Nanda","name":"Nanda, Indrajit","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Konstantinos","key":"evangeloupau","last":"Evangelou","name":"Evangelou, Konstantinos","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Teodora","key":"nikolovapau","last":"Nikolova","name":"Nikolova, Teodora","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Veronika","key":"hornpau","last":"Horn","name":"Horn, Veronika","rank":5,"